  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This 2025 Journal of Inorganic Biochemistry article is already showing early scholarly uptake, with 2 OpenAlex citations and downstream reuse documented in 3 records. Its citation trail includes a Nature piece and a follow-up in the same journal, suggesting the structural and mechanistic framing is being noticed beyond a single niche.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Avalos and Drennan focus on the cobalamin-binding domain of cobalamin-dependent radical S-adenosylmethionine enzymes, a topic at the intersection of enzyme structure, cofactors, and challenging radical chemistry. The paper sits squarely in the structural biochemistry tradition associated with the Drennan group, and the record shows support from NIH/NIGMS funding and Howard Hughes Medical Institute stewardship. In other words, this is a carefully positioned mechanistic article from a well-established research program rather than a one-off observation.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest impact signal is still early, but it is concrete. OpenAlex records 2 citations, both in 2026, and the citing venues indicate visibility in two different scholarly contexts: one in Nature and one in the Journal of Inorganic Biochemistry. That mix points to recognition both within the immediate field and in a broader high-profile scientific outlet. The citing works themselves span topics including enzyme structure and vitamin B12 chemistry, which is consistent with the paper\u2019s focus on how cobalamin-dependent radical enzymes organize and use their cofactors.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Beyond formal citations, the reuse graph shows 3 downstream reuse events, including an article mention indexed by Europe PMC. That is a modest but meaningful sign that the work is circulating in related literature and structured biomedical metadata channels. At the same time, the wider public-discourse record is quiet in the sources available here, so the evidence supports scholarly and infrastructural uptake more than broad public attention at this stage.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, the article appears to be an early but credible reference point for researchers studying cobalamin-dependent radical SAM enzymes and protein-cofactor architecture. The available record emphasizes disciplinary relevance, selective uptake in authoritative venues, and the kind of incremental reuse that often precedes a larger citation footprint. With only 2 citations, the story is still emerging, but the direction of travel is clear.</p>",
